{
  "image_name": "9d7818b673866cac1ce6571ebdcc02ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes catalyst and reactor design across increasing length scales, from atomic sites to full electrochemical reactors. On the left, “atomic design” shows isolated single-atom catalytic sites anchored on a support. The idea is that these active atoms selectively convert a target contaminant into a desired product, emphasizing control at the smallest scale.\n\nAt the intermediate “microstructure design” level, two strategies are illustrated. A porous flow-through architecture lets contaminants pass directly through channels containing catalytic material, improving contact and transport. A layered composite structure instead appears to use a surface coating or membrane-like top layer over a support, helping direct reactions and suppress interference from competing contaminants.\n\nOn the right, “reactor design” expands this to device-scale configurations. Simple flow-by and flow-through electrode arrangements are shown, followed by larger electrode stacks and packed or fluidized beds made of many catalytic particles or electrodes. Together, these designs suggest a progression from tuning active sites, to shaping transport pathways in materials, to arranging electrodes and beds for scalable treatment performance. A horizontal axis at the bottom indicates this multiscale progression from about nanometers to decimeters/meters.",
